    {
      "id": "t003079",
      "source": "Sustainability is a balance between renewal and extraction over time.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003080",
      "source": "Formal model.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003081",
      "source": "Stock-flow model with regeneration and harvest; test non-decreasing stock.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003082",
      "source": "Problem 170. Regeneration and harvest: case 5",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003083",
      "source": "Knowledge context.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003084",
      "source": "Renewable does not mean unlimited. A resource can decline when use exceeds regeneration.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003085",
      "source": "Given facts.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003086",
      "source": "A forest resource starts at 110 units. Each year it regenerates 20 units and people harvest 21 units. Assume these amounts stay constant for 3 years and no other changes occur.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003087",
      "source": "Rules.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003088",
      "source": "Annual net change = regeneration − harvest. In this simplified model, use is sustainable over the period if stock does not decline.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003089",
      "source": "Task.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003090",
      "source": "What is the stock after 3 years, and is the harvest sustainable under this classroom definition?",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003091",
      "source": "Step-by-step solution.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003092",
      "source": "1. Annual net change=20−21=-1. 2. Three-year change=-3. 3. Final stock=110+(-3)=107. 4. Compare final stock with starting stock.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003093",
      "source": "Answer.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003094",
      "source": "107 units; not sustainable under the stated definition.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003095",
      "source": "Knowledge takeaway.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003096",
      "source": "Sustainability is a balance between renewal and extraction over time.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003097",
      "source": "Formal model.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003098",
      "source": "Stock-flow model with regeneration and harvest; test non-decreasing stock.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003099",
      "source": "N10. Migration and population balance",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003100",
      "source": "Problem 171. Population balance in a town: case 1",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003101",
      "source": "Knowledge context.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003102",
      "source": "Population changes through births, deaths, immigration, and emigration.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003103",
      "source": "Given facts.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003104",
      "source": "A town begins the year with 1100 residents. During the year: births=20, deaths=11, people moving in=32, people moving out=25.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003105",
      "source": "Rules.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003106",
      "source": "End population = start + births − deaths + in-migration − out-migration. Natural change and migration change are separate components.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003107",
      "source": "Task.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003108",
      "source": "Compute natural change, migration change, and the year-end population.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003109",
      "source": "Step-by-step solution.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003110",
      "source": "1. Natural change=20−11=9. 2. Migration change=32−25=7. 3. Total change=9+7=16. 4. End population=1100+16=1116.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003111",
      "source": "Answer.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003112",
      "source": "Natural change +9; migration change +7; end population 1116.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003113",
      "source": "Knowledge takeaway.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003114",
      "source": "Breaking total change into components helps explain why a population grew or shrank.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003115",
      "source": "Formal model.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003116",
      "source": "Balance equation P1=P0+births−deaths+in−out.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
