      "source": "Event A lasted from year 180 to 215. Event B lasted from 190 to 218. Treat intervals as continuous for this exercise.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003259",
      "source": "Rules.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003260",
      "source": "Two intervals overlap when max(start times) < min(end times). Overlap length = min(end times) − max(start times), if positive.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003261",
      "source": "Task.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003262",
      "source": "Did the events overlap? If so, for how many years?",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003263",
      "source": "Step-by-step solution.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003264",
      "source": "1. Later start=max(180,190)=190. 2. Earlier end=min(215,218)=215. 3. Difference=215−190=25.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003265",
      "source": "Answer.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003266",
      "source": "Yes, overlap 25 years.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003267",
      "source": "Knowledge takeaway.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003268",
      "source": "Interval reasoning distinguishes “before/after” from “overlapping in time.”",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003269",
      "source": "Formal model.",
      "translation": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "t003270",
      "source": "Interval intersection; compute max starts and min ends.",
      "translation": ""
    },
